WEBVTT

00:01.399 --> 00:04.433
This is what one participant
saw, through their own eyes.

00:04.963 --> 00:09.611
The robot has picked up a piece, and stopped.
It's still holding it. Nothing moves.

00:10.241 --> 00:12.638
The robot has no idea anything is wrong.

00:13.168 --> 00:16.593
But follow the glowing dot. That's
where the person is looking.

00:16.623 --> 00:19.280
Their eyes have already
started asking questions.

00:19.910 --> 00:21.728
So what if the robot could read that?

00:27.358 --> 00:32.018
Robots that pick and place objects rely
on sensing, planning, and movement.

00:32.048 --> 00:35.708
When any one of them fails,
the robot often doesn't notice.

00:36.238 --> 00:40.922
In our study, the robot failed in three
ways. It froze in the middle of a move.

00:40.952 --> 00:42.952
It put a piece in the wrong place.

00:42.982 --> 00:46.473
Or it closed its gripper on
nothing, and carried on anyway.

00:47.003 --> 00:52.112
When you're working side by side with a robot,
a missed failure doesn't just stall the task.

00:52.142 --> 00:55.072
It breaks the rhythm between
you, and it costs trust.

00:56.302 --> 01:02.504
Fifty-one people solved six Tangram puzzles with
a TIAGo robot, taking turns to place the pieces.

01:02.934 --> 01:07.777
They wore eye-tracking glasses that recorded
where they looked, two hundred times a second.

01:08.207 --> 01:13.000
In total, we captured one thousand, one
hundred and eighty-two robot actions.

01:13.030 --> 01:16.069
One hundred and forty-nine
of them were failures.

01:17.499 --> 01:19.671
We divided the scene into six regions:

01:19.701 --> 01:24.015
the board, the gripper, the robot's
head and body, and the two piece trays.

01:24.445 --> 01:28.889
Then we turned looking into numbers.
How long people dwelled on each region.

01:28.919 --> 01:33.362
How often they switched between them.
How unpredictable those switches were.

01:33.392 --> 01:35.834
And how many long, fixed stares they made.

01:36.264 --> 01:39.811
Each number is also compared
with that person's own history.

01:39.841 --> 01:44.879
A long look at the gripper is routine for
some people, and a warning sign for others.

01:45.409 --> 01:50.884
Here's a normal move. And here's the freeze.
Same person, same robot. Very different eyes.

01:55.114 --> 02:00.133
Lightweight classifiers read these patterns
at three checkpoints in every action:

02:00.163 --> 02:03.998
after transport, after placement,
and after the robot returns.

02:05.028 --> 02:09.187
But eyes wander for many reasons.
Getting ready for your own turn.

02:09.217 --> 02:14.392
Surprise at a move that was actually fine.
Or simply glancing at the robot's face.

02:14.822 --> 02:21.004
Tuned to catch ninety percent of failures, gaze
alone raised more than four hundred and fifty alerts.

02:21.034 --> 02:23.279
Seven in every ten were false alarms.

02:24.709 --> 02:26.988
Vision-language models work differently.

02:27.018 --> 02:33.743
Show one a sequence of images, and it can reason about
whether something went wrong, and what kind of failure it was.

02:34.173 --> 02:37.063
But every question costs
computation and time.

02:37.093 --> 02:41.824
Checking every action means one thousand,
one hundred and eighty-two calls.

02:41.854 --> 02:45.316
Checking at every stage means
more than three thousand.

02:46.746 --> 02:48.731
So we let each one do what it does best.

02:49.161 --> 02:53.607
Gaze is the lookout. It watches
continuously, and costs almost nothing.

02:54.037 --> 02:57.484
Only when gaze raises an alert
do we call in the expert:

02:57.514 --> 03:02.326
the vision-language model inspects the
visual evidence, and gives its verdict.

03:03.156 --> 03:10.793
For the freeze, it saw the robot holding the triangle at the
pickup point, with no visible progress for more than eight seconds.

03:10.823 --> 03:12.350
Verdict: freezing failure.

03:13.780 --> 03:20.496
That freeze was flagged by gaze during transport, and confirmed
straight away, well before the action would have finished.

03:21.526 --> 03:23.559
The wrong placement was trickier.

03:23.589 --> 03:29.248
Gaze flagged it during transport, but the
mistake wasn't visible yet, so the model said no.

03:29.278 --> 03:33.687
At placement, gaze flagged it again.
This time, the evidence was there.

03:34.717 --> 03:37.914
The empty grasp stayed quiet
until the robot returned.

03:37.944 --> 03:41.141
Gaze caught it late, but the
model still confirmed it.

03:42.771 --> 03:49.050
Across all the robot's actions, the vision-language
model filtered out almost every false gaze alert.

03:49.080 --> 03:53.172
Of three hundred and twenty-two
false alarms, only three survived.

03:54.002 --> 03:57.711
Precision jumped from under thirty
percent, to ninety-seven and a half.

03:58.541 --> 04:07.202
And it needed sixty-one percent fewer model calls than checking every
action, with an F1 score of point eight eight, compared with point nine two.

04:08.132 --> 04:10.710
Checking stage by stage, the savings grow:

04:10.740 --> 04:18.286
seventy-five percent fewer calls, with more than two thirds
of all failures caught before the robot had finished its move.

04:19.216 --> 04:20.190
There is a price.

04:20.220 --> 04:25.035
Some real failures slipped past the gaze
screen, and the model turned down others.

04:25.065 --> 04:27.516
Recall fell from ninety percent to eighty.

04:28.846 --> 04:33.495
So what gave the failures away? Long,
fixed stares at the gripper and the board.

04:33.525 --> 04:37.531
And looking at the board more, or
less, than that person usually did.

04:38.761 --> 04:44.516
Next, we're testing whether this carries over to
unseen puzzles, and to an entirely different task.

04:45.146 --> 04:49.777
Because a robot doesn't need to watch everything.
It just needs to know where we looked.
